this was the moment of arrest  jimmy ly put into an unmarked police van  outside his home in hong kong  then driven away  raided the publisher's office late in  the morning  he was then later brought to the offices  of his media company  next media the paper itself released  these pictures  mr lie being escorted around their  office by police officers  police said they were looking for  evidence but did not specify  what exactly the 71 year old jimmy ly  is among several arrested in a police  operation focused on his group of media  outlets  including the popular tabloid apple  daily  in a statement police said seven people  have been detained on suspicion of fraud  and colluding with foreign forces part  of the new security law  this morning around 9 45 together with  our colleagues from the criminal  investigation department  and military staff we got a warrant to  conduct an operation  to search for evidence our operation so  far smooth and we hope to finish soon  and not to disturb the operation of the  media  it is not the first time jimmy ly has  been arrested  china's state media has portrayed him as  a traitor and accused him of being one  of the masterminds behind the many  recent protests  in the city he is a well-known and  outspoken pro-democracy figure in hong  kong  who regularly criticize china's  authoritarian rule  many here in hong kong seem as an  unlikely hero  i don't think the police should be doing  this after all this is a media  organization  hong kong claims to have freedom of the  press so i don't think they should be  raiding the media's premises  the self-made billionaire and media  owner is the only high-profile business  person  who so far has been willing to openly  criticize beijing  the arrests come only days after the u.s  government announced sanctions on hong  kong and mainland chinese officials  it's a clear signal that china is  determined to enforce  the new security law despite growing  international pressure  well joining me now from london is  nathan law the chairman of  hong kong's democristo party he faces  similar  charges in hong kong and is threatened  with arrest if he  returns nathan what do you make of jimmy  lizarest  well this is definitely a classic signal  from the communist party saying that um  they would arbitrarily and are basically  using  uh the the the national security law to  prosecute any political attackers that  they don't like  so uh jim eli has been very vocal in  criticizing the  chinese government and protecting hong  kong people's freedom so that would  set up a case and to threaten the others  to be silent  well jimmy jimmy you say has been very  very vocal you also have been part of  these  uh protests is he as beijing claims the  mastermind behind this  the protest well of course of course not  um the way beijing portrayed that is to  frame these very prominent for this  mattress to take full responsibility  for all the um well upheaval  for the past year but actually this is a  leaderless movement um  people are acting on their behalf and  really devote themselves into the  movement with their own capacity  so um i don't think that is the reality  they just wanted  to cook up cases to prosecute prominent  activities  so you say the movement is a leaderless  one but how  is this high-profile arrest going to  affect the pro-democracy movement  well it would definitely create chilling  effects which means that um  even though like you are not a  so-called colluding with foreign forces  you are acting on your own  willingness but beijing could definitely  traumatic cases on you  and they will scare off of people who  still wanted to voice out in hong kong  so that is  beijing's agenda and i urge the public  not to be scared of  really giving out your voice are you  yourself i mean you're in exile if you  will in london right now are you  yourself scared  well of course we understand how far  reaching china's rich could be  and even though we are kind of scared  but  it's our choice to um adopt what action  to cope with it  my actions would be speaking louder and  louder to be  able to talk hong kong people's demands  and their genuine thoughts  overseas in order to get more  international attention and support  nathan are you going back to hong kong  anytime soon  well according to the state media i am  a wanted uh fugitive so uh it is  unlikely that i  am able to go back to hong kong but if i  do in the future  then it will be hong kong becomes a  democracy and free play  nathan law thank you very much  i know the law has already left hong  kong and many others are planning to do  the same the  number of residents leaving is at its  highest in seven years according to  official figures  fears that the territories freedoms are  being eroded have forced people  to consider their options gary  and his fiancee have just a few more  months to enjoy their life in hong kong  before they say goodbye to their  hometown and emigrate to australia  when gary filed the application five  years ago  leaving was only a backup plan since  then  hong kong has been through massive  social and political upheaval  but it was the unprecedented  anti-government protest last year  that finally pushed him over the edge  this isn't the hong kong i grew up with  anymore freedom has been restricted  hong kong will soon be no different from  the rest of china  i feel even more unsafe under the new  national security law  we've lost hope here so we have to find  it elsewhere  to fight or to flee that's a tough  question  for many hong kongers like gary starting  a life abroad will likely be a rocky  path  even in his job as an engineer gary and  his fiancee  who does not want to be interviewed  because she fears for her job here  are prepared to start from scratch it's  a price  they're willing to pay in exchange for  liberty  it's tough to abandon our well-paid jobs  friends  everything we've established here where  we were born and raised  we did struggle to make this decision  but around a third of my friends are  also considering  leaving or at least sending their  children abroad  factors show that the number of hong  kong residents living  is at its highest in seven years around  30 000 people left in 2019  a 40 search in just a year  and that's expected to keep growing  since the new chinese security law was  enacted last month  a number of countries have pledged to  make it easier for hong kongers to  emigrate  australia taiwan and britain are among  the popular destinations  the uk government will even allow nearly  the 3 million  hong kongers with british national  overseas passports  a document from the colonial era to  settle there  emigration is more than a personal  choice in hong kong it's also a  diplomatic issue  beijing has wanted to stop recognizing  the special british passports of some of  hong kong's residents  in retaliation for britain's offer to  protect them in  some probation heavyweights even  advocate revoking the permanent  residencies of those granted uk  citizenship  which means they could lose the right to  vote and stand for election in hong kong  gary has recently been traveling around  hong kong  to capture its iconic scenery these  pictures  will soon just be memories of the place  he will no longer  call home  especially since many friends will soon  drift apart i treasure the remaining  chances to be together  and i hope i can still contribute to my  hometown by other means  in the future  joining me now from hong kong is  pro-democracy activist  and former secretary general of the  political party demo sister  joshua wong joshua welcome what is your  reaction  to the arrest of jimmy lye  with the threat of beijing which is hard  for us to guarantee our personal safety  and with the crackdown and the mass  arrest conduct with the national  security legislation  we just proved that how the children  effect is being used to silence other  voice  what is the message beijing is trying to  deliver  to the pro-democracy movement in hong  kong with such a high profile arrest  we have no intention to have any  collusion with the foreign force  we have never caught on extension  since drivers after national security  law is implemented  but now beijing just provided misleading  propaganda  and just try to generate more and more  excuse to silence our voice  and to crack down on the protest but no  matter what happened  we're just urging beijing to hold the  accountability  to stop the erosion of universal value  including press freedom and also a free  election that all we fight for  but joshua how successful are you all  being really in your attempts i mean you  as a candidate have been barred from  standing in these elections which  themselves have now been postponed  is it having any impact on beijing  beijing could not censor us out from the  democracy movement they can kick us out  from the office  they can censor us out from balance but  they can't disqualify  our commitment to fight for freedom and  continue to engage in the democracy  movement  you are committed to engaging with the  democracy movement but speaking about  the democratic processes in hong kong  they have been postponed at least for a  year when it comes to the legislative  council  elections the government said it was  because of the coronavirus pandemic  do you believe the government  i believe there's no no ground  to expect the election will still  continue instead of saying they  try to postpone the election now is they  hope they  try to cancel the whole election at all  but do you do you believe their  reasoning that it's because of the  coronavirus pandemic and therefore it  was unsafe  to hold these elections  uh with the threat and the risks  generated by the authorities which is  hard for us to  ensure everything but i think now is the  time for the  world to put hong kong under the global  spotlight  but hong kong in a way is being put  under the global spotlight i mean you  have sanctions that have been imposed  from the united states against carrie  lam for instance and some other  officials  do you think that support is working for  the pro-democracy movement in hong kong  uh we are not only focusing on the move  or the announcement from the united  states i think how country  uh in europe react on the crackdown in  hong kong  would be also remarkable for us to  realize that  it's time to hold the aging  accountability  do you think europe is doing enough to  hold beijing accountable  a different country might have different  foreign policy towards hong kong and  china but no matter  what their original approach and was the  consideration  it's time to reassess house their policy  to continue  enhance uh our fights  and also how they uh really assess their  foreign policy  to the hotline crackdown from beijing  i'd like to talk about you i mean you uh  disbanded your political party  democristo around the time that the new  security law  was being implemented you said you  feared being targeted  by beijing as a result of the new  security law do you still  have those fears  um which is hard for us to ensure  um our safety and i'm not sure when i  will be  arrested and being extradited to china  and we might be all worried about  the threats that we need to face in the  upcoming future but no matter what  happen  we still have no regret at all to  continue  course on free election some of your  colleagues for example  nathan law and others have left hong  kong  to try and bring hong kong's voice to  the  international arena do you plan on  leaving hong kong joshua  will continue to plan to fight for  freedom and that's my decision  joshua wong pro democracy activist  speaking to us from hong kong thank you  very much for your time  you

